1
00:00:00,950 --> 00:00:06,116
Sunday, July first, twenty eighteen. Late morning. Reau prison,

2
00:00:06,166 --> 00:00:09,676
about forty kilometers south east of Paris.

3
00:00:11,476 --> 00:00:12,719
In the visiting room,

4
00:00:12,769 --> 00:00:16,781
one of France's most famous prisoners is sitting with his brother.

5
00:00:16,831 --> 00:00:20,843
His name is Redoine Faid. He is serving decades for armed robbery,

6
00:00:20,893 --> 00:00:24,475
and for a shootout in which a young policewoman was killed.

7
00:00:26,275 --> 00:00:27,944
At around eleven thirty,

8
00:00:27,994 --> 00:00:31,886
a small white helicopter appears over the prison walls.

9
00:00:31,936 --> 00:00:36,042
It drops down into an inner courtyard. Armed men jump out.

10
00:00:36,092 --> 00:00:37,619
Smoke grenades go off.

11
00:00:37,669 --> 00:00:42,349
Someone starts cutting through a steel door with an angle grinder.

12
00:00:44,149 --> 00:00:49,294
Minutes later, the helicopter lifts off again. With Redoine Faid on board.

13
00:00:52,594 --> 00:00:56,464
No shots were fired. Nobody was hurt. At his trial,

14
00:00:56,514 --> 00:01:00,614
the prosecutor gave the exact length of the operation.

15
00:01:00,664 --> 00:01:03,612
Seven minutes and thirty three seconds.

16
00:01:06,612 --> 00:01:10,550
It was not the first time Faid had escaped from a French prison.

17
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:11,734
Five years earlier,

18
00:01:11,784 --> 00:01:15,472
he had blown his way out through five doors with explosives.

19
00:01:17,272 --> 00:01:21,770
This is the story of the man who turned his favorite crime films into a career.

20
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:26,202
And of the seven and a half minutes that humiliated the French prison system.

21
00:01:29,952 --> 00:01:32,183
Chapter 1. Seven minutes.

22
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:40,946
To understand what happened that Sunday,

23
00:01:40,996 --> 00:01:43,287
you have to start a few hours earlier,

24
00:01:43,337 --> 00:01:45,751
at a small airfield in the Paris region.

25
00:01:47,551 --> 00:01:48,885
According to reports,

26
00:01:48,935 --> 00:01:53,567
men posing as flight students had arranged a lesson with an instructor,

27
00:01:53,617 --> 00:01:56,468
a helicopter pilot. Once they were with him,

28
00:01:56,518 --> 00:01:59,634
the men pulled out weapons and took him hostage.

29
00:01:59,684 --> 00:02:02,337
He was ordered to fly south, toward Reau.

30
00:02:04,137 --> 00:02:09,402
The helicopter was an Alouette two. A small, old, French made machine,

31
00:02:09,452 --> 00:02:13,274
first built in the nineteen fifties. Light, simple,

32
00:02:13,324 --> 00:02:15,324
and very easy to recognize.

33
00:02:17,124 --> 00:02:18,813
Reau was not an old prison.

34
00:02:18,863 --> 00:02:22,676
It was one of the newest in France, opened in twenty eleven,

35
00:02:22,726 --> 00:02:25,638
and designed for nearly eight hundred inmates.

36
00:02:25,688 --> 00:02:28,793
Much of it was protected by anti helicopter nets,

37
00:02:28,843 --> 00:02:33,815
steel wires stretched over the courtyards to stop exactly this kind of attack.

38
00:02:35,615 --> 00:02:41,417
The prison had been inaugurated by President Nicolas Sarkozy in September twenty eleven.

39
00:02:41,467 --> 00:02:42,880
It held men and women,

40
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:47,934
from short sentences to some of the most dangerous prisoners in the country.

41
00:02:47,984 --> 00:02:51,725
On paper, it was one of the most secure places in France.

42
00:02:53,525 --> 00:02:56,722
In twenty twenty one, according to official figures,

43
00:02:56,772 --> 00:02:59,783
it held around six hundred and seventy prisoners.

44
00:02:59,833 --> 00:03:02,093
It had its own maximum security wing,

45
00:03:02,143 --> 00:03:05,903
and its own national assessment center for long term inmates.

46
00:03:07,703 --> 00:03:10,603
But not all of it. According to prison unions,

47
00:03:10,653 --> 00:03:15,284
the pilot was directed to the one courtyard that was not covered by nets.

48
00:03:15,334 --> 00:03:20,607
A union representative later told the press that inmates never used that courtyard.

49
00:03:20,657 --> 00:03:22,274
Except, he said, to leave.

50
00:03:24,074 --> 00:03:28,912
The helicopter landed. According to the French justice minister at the time,

51
00:03:28,962 --> 00:03:30,005
Nicole Belloubet,

52
00:03:30,055 --> 00:03:33,543
two of the commandos went into the prison to find Faid,

53
00:03:33,593 --> 00:03:36,115
while a third man stayed with the pilot.

54
00:03:37,915 --> 00:03:42,436
They wore balaclavas, and carried assault rifles. They set off smoke grenades,

55
00:03:42,486 --> 00:03:45,248
so that the security cameras could not see them.

56
00:03:45,298 --> 00:03:50,229
And they used an angle grinder to cut through the doors leading to the visiting area.

57
00:03:52,029 --> 00:03:55,940
Faid was waiting. He walked out with them, crossed the courtyard,

58
00:03:55,990 --> 00:03:57,890
and climbed into the helicopter.

59
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:00,511
The guards inside the prison are not armed.

60
00:04:00,561 --> 00:04:02,095
They could do very little.

61
00:04:03,895 --> 00:04:07,969
The helicopter took off and flew north, over the Paris region.

62
00:04:08,019 --> 00:04:12,625
It landed near Gonesse, not far from Charles de Gaulle airport. There,

63
00:04:12,675 --> 00:04:16,549
the men set it on fire, switched to a car, and disappeared.

64
00:04:16,599 --> 00:04:19,941
The pilot was released, unharmed, but badly shaken.

65
00:04:21,741 --> 00:04:24,637
The whole operation, from landing to take off,

66
00:04:24,687 --> 00:04:27,519
had taken just over seven and a half minutes.

67
00:04:30,519 --> 00:04:33,230
Within hours, a huge manhunt had begun.

68
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:37,902
And reporters started asking two questions. How could this happen?

69
00:04:37,952 --> 00:04:40,168
And who exactly is Redoine Faid?

70
00:04:42,418 --> 00:04:44,718
Chapter 2. The boy from Creil.

71
00:04:51,068 --> 00:04:55,819
Redoine Faid was born on May tenth, nineteen seventy two, in Creil,

72
00:04:55,869 --> 00:04:58,828
a town in the Oise region, north of Paris.

73
00:04:58,878 --> 00:05:01,264
His parents had come from Algeria.

74
00:05:01,314 --> 00:05:03,342
He was one of a large family,

75
00:05:03,392 --> 00:05:06,853
the second youngest of many brothers and sisters.

76
00:05:08,653 --> 00:05:11,208
He grew up in the housing estates of Creil,

77
00:05:11,258 --> 00:05:15,630
in a neighborhood called Guynemer. His father worked at a chemical plant.

78
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:18,598
His mother died of leukemia when he was nineteen.

79
00:05:20,398 --> 00:05:23,334
From an early age, he loved gangster films.

80
00:05:23,384 --> 00:05:26,806
And one film in particular. Heat, by Michael Mann,

81
00:05:26,856 --> 00:05:29,098
released in nineteen ninety five,

82
00:05:29,148 --> 00:05:35,626
with Robert De Niro as a professional thief who plans every robbery like a military operation.

83
00:05:37,426 --> 00:05:40,204
Faid later said he watched it again and again.

84
00:05:40,254 --> 00:05:44,139
He studied the way the robbers moved, the way they communicated,

85
00:05:44,189 --> 00:05:48,259
the way they planned their escape routes. He also admired Scarface,

86
00:05:48,309 --> 00:05:52,009
Reservoir Dogs, and the real French gangster Jacques Mesrine,

87
00:05:52,059 --> 00:05:55,637
who escaped from several prisons in the nineteen seventies.

88
00:05:57,437 --> 00:06:00,721
Years later, according to French and British press,

89
00:06:00,771 --> 00:06:03,793
Faid met Michael Mann at a film event in Paris.

90
00:06:03,843 --> 00:06:07,911
He reportedly told the director, You were my technical adviser.

91
00:06:10,161 --> 00:06:12,136
Chapter 3. The robber.

92
00:06:18,486 --> 00:06:22,866
Faid's career in crime started early. According to French accounts,

93
00:06:22,916 --> 00:06:25,974
in nineteen ninety, while still in high school,

94
00:06:26,024 --> 00:06:28,817
he took part in a robbery of a bank branch.

95
00:06:28,867 --> 00:06:31,462
It was his first time in police custody.

96
00:06:33,262 --> 00:06:36,445
In the mid nineteen nineties, his crew grew bolder.

97
00:06:36,495 --> 00:06:37,966
In one robbery in Creil,

98
00:06:38,016 --> 00:06:41,580
they reportedly copied a scene from the film Point Break,

99
00:06:41,630 --> 00:06:44,179
wearing masks of politicians. In another,

100
00:06:44,229 --> 00:06:47,222
they held the family of a bank director hostage,

101
00:06:47,272 --> 00:06:49,314
and forced him to open the vault.

102
00:06:51,114 --> 00:06:53,825
In that robbery, according to French accounts,

103
00:06:53,875 --> 00:06:57,187
one of the robbers was even dressed as Father Christmas.

104
00:06:57,237 --> 00:06:58,988
It was crime as a performance.

105
00:06:59,038 --> 00:07:01,210
And Faid was learning how to plan it.

106
00:07:03,010 --> 00:07:07,227
Then they moved on to armored vans. In July nineteen ninety seven,

107
00:07:07,277 --> 00:07:11,431
in Villepinte, near Paris, Faid's gang attacked a cash transport.

108
00:07:11,481 --> 00:07:13,888
They got away with millions of francs.

109
00:07:13,938 --> 00:07:16,086
But Faid was shot in the shoulder.

110
00:07:16,136 --> 00:07:19,513
And the blood he left behind gave the police his DNA.

111
00:07:21,313 --> 00:07:24,476
The haul was about two point seven million francs,

112
00:07:24,526 --> 00:07:26,789
roughly four hundred thousand euros.

113
00:07:26,839 --> 00:07:29,488
For a young man from the estates of Creil,

114
00:07:29,538 --> 00:07:30,580
it was a fortune.

115
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:36,596
In September nineteen ninety eight, he was arrested in Switzerland,

116
00:07:36,646 --> 00:07:40,672
carrying false papers. According to French accounts, he escaped,

117
00:07:40,722 --> 00:07:43,155
was caught again and taken to hospital,

118
00:07:43,205 --> 00:07:45,830
and then escaped a second time. Even then,

119
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:47,549
he did not like being held.

120
00:07:49,349 --> 00:07:52,990
He went on the run abroad. Accounts differ on how long.

121
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:57,218
In his own telling, he spent time in Switzerland and in Israel,

122
00:07:57,268 --> 00:08:02,653
where he claimed to have learned Hebrew and disguised himself as an Orthodox Jew.

123
00:08:02,703 --> 00:08:06,881
Those details come from his own memoir, and are hard to verify.

124
00:08:08,681 --> 00:08:12,626
In December nineteen ninety eight, he was arrested in Paris,

125
00:08:12,676 --> 00:08:16,821
and sent to prison. He would spend about ten years behind bars.

126
00:08:18,621 --> 00:08:22,938
In March two thousand nine, Redoine Faid was released on parole.

127
00:08:22,988 --> 00:08:24,985
And he seemed to have changed.

128
00:08:26,785 --> 00:08:31,239
In twenty ten, he published a memoir, written with a crime journalist.

129
00:08:31,289 --> 00:08:33,427
Its title, in English, was Robber.

130
00:08:33,477 --> 00:08:36,258
From the Housing Estates to Organized Crime.

131
00:08:36,308 --> 00:08:40,633
He went on national television to promote it. He spoke about prison,

132
00:08:40,683 --> 00:08:44,171
about the estates, about the films that had shaped him.

133
00:08:45,971 --> 00:08:49,392
Years later, the book was even translated into English,

134
00:08:49,442 --> 00:08:50,844
under the title Outlaw.

135
00:08:52,644 --> 00:08:56,147
On one show, he said his demons were completely dead.

136
00:08:56,197 --> 00:08:59,164
He was a reformed man, he said. A writer now.

137
00:09:00,964 --> 00:09:03,892
Many viewers believed him. The police did not.

138
00:09:07,642 --> 00:09:10,151
Chapter 4. Villiers-sur-Marne.

139
00:09:16,501 --> 00:09:18,443
On May twentieth, twenty ten,

140
00:09:18,493 --> 00:09:23,732
a gang of hooded men attacked a cash transport vehicle in Villiers sur Marne,

141
00:09:23,782 --> 00:09:24,694
east of Paris.

142
00:09:26,494 --> 00:09:31,466
Local municipal police arrived. A gunfight broke out on a busy road.

143
00:09:31,516 --> 00:09:37,079
A twenty six year old municipal police officer, Aurelie Fouquet, was killed.

144
00:09:37,129 --> 00:09:38,999
Her colleague was wounded.

145
00:09:40,799 --> 00:09:42,102
Her death shocked France,

146
00:09:42,152 --> 00:09:46,054
and turned the case into one of the most closely followed in the country.

147
00:09:47,854 --> 00:09:50,834
Investigators linked the gang to Redoine Faid.

148
00:09:50,884 --> 00:09:53,402
They believed he was not just involved,

149
00:09:53,452 --> 00:09:56,827
but that he had organized it. In June twenty eleven,

150
00:09:56,877 --> 00:09:58,276
he was arrested again.

151
00:10:00,076 --> 00:10:02,927
The case went to trial in twenty sixteen.

152
00:10:02,977 --> 00:10:08,163
Faid was sentenced to eighteen years. On appeal, in April twenty eighteen,

153
00:10:08,213 --> 00:10:11,489
a court in Paris found him to be the organizer,

154
00:10:11,539 --> 00:10:14,885
and increased the sentence to twenty five years.

155
00:10:16,685 --> 00:10:19,638
His brother Faycal was also convicted in the case.

156
00:10:19,688 --> 00:10:23,180
But he was in Algeria, and France could not bring him back.

157
00:10:23,230 --> 00:10:24,982
He was tried there separately.

158
00:10:26,782 --> 00:10:31,627
He was also convicted for another armored van robbery, in northern France,

159
00:10:31,677 --> 00:10:36,257
in twenty eleven. On appeal, that sentence reached twenty eight years.

160
00:10:38,057 --> 00:10:42,046
At the first trial for that robbery, in October twenty seventeen,

161
00:10:42,096 --> 00:10:44,221
he was sentenced to eighteen years.

162
00:10:44,271 --> 00:10:48,136
His sentences were starting to stack up, one on top of another.

163
00:10:49,936 --> 00:10:56,615
The man who had told television audiences that his demons were dead was now facing the rest of his life in prison.

164
00:10:56,665 --> 00:10:58,680
And he had no intention of staying.

165
00:11:02,130 --> 00:11:04,466
Chapter 5. Five doors.

166
00:11:10,816 --> 00:11:14,259
On the morning of April thirteenth, twenty thirteen,

167
00:11:14,309 --> 00:11:18,290
Redoine Faid was being held at the prison of Lille Sequedin,

168
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:19,566
in northern France.

169
00:11:21,366 --> 00:11:26,297
Somehow, he had obtained a gun, and explosives. According to French accounts,

170
00:11:26,347 --> 00:11:28,949
the gun had been hidden in a laundry bag.

171
00:11:30,749 --> 00:11:33,548
That morning, he took four people hostage,

172
00:11:33,598 --> 00:11:37,415
and used them as human shields. Then he blew his way out.

173
00:11:37,465 --> 00:11:42,435
One door after another. Five doors in total, blasted open with explosives,

174
00:11:42,485 --> 00:11:46,098
until he reached the outside, where a car was waiting.

175
00:11:47,898 --> 00:11:50,511
The getaway car was later found burned.

176
00:11:50,561 --> 00:11:53,924
A European arrest warrant was issued the same day.

177
00:11:53,974 --> 00:11:57,338
Interpol said Faid was trying to get false papers,

178
00:11:57,388 --> 00:11:59,318
possibly to leave for Israel.

179
00:12:01,118 --> 00:12:04,522
In the end, it was the false papers that betrayed him.

180
00:12:04,572 --> 00:12:06,378
According to French accounts,

181
00:12:06,428 --> 00:12:11,048
investigators tracked the phone of a man selling fake identity documents.

182
00:12:11,098 --> 00:12:12,584
And it led them to Faid.

183
00:12:14,384 --> 00:12:16,711
He stayed free for forty six days.

184
00:12:16,761 --> 00:12:20,626
In the early hours of May twenty ninth, twenty thirteen,

185
00:12:20,676 --> 00:12:24,541
police found him in a budget hotel in Pontault Combault,

186
00:12:24,591 --> 00:12:25,519
east of Paris.

187
00:12:27,319 --> 00:12:31,932
For the Sequedin escape, he was later sentenced to another ten years.

188
00:12:31,982 --> 00:12:33,757
He was placed in isolation,

189
00:12:33,807 --> 00:12:38,420
in the high security wing of Fleury Merogis, France's largest prison.

190
00:12:40,220 --> 00:12:43,033
At that trial, in March twenty seventeen,

191
00:12:43,083 --> 00:12:46,595
prosecutors had asked for twelve to fourteen years.

192
00:12:46,645 --> 00:12:49,109
The court gave him ten. He appealed.

193
00:12:50,909 --> 00:12:54,666
The French prison service now knew exactly how dangerous he was.

194
00:12:54,716 --> 00:12:55,677
Five years later,

195
00:12:55,727 --> 00:12:58,770
it would learn that it still had not understood him.

196
00:13:02,220 --> 00:13:04,567
Chapter 6. The weak spot.

197
00:13:10,917 --> 00:13:14,372
By twenty eighteen, Faid was being held at Reau.

198
00:13:14,422 --> 00:13:16,854
His sentences added up to decades.

199
00:13:16,904 --> 00:13:19,701
And he was still allowed family visits.

200
00:13:21,501 --> 00:13:25,406
Looking back, there had been signs. According to French reports,

201
00:13:25,456 --> 00:13:30,165
drones had been seen flying over the prison several months before the escape.

202
00:13:30,215 --> 00:13:36,159
Investigators later suspected they were reconnaissance flights, mapping the courtyards, the nets,

203
00:13:36,209 --> 00:13:37,518
and the routes inside.

204
00:13:39,318 --> 00:13:40,922
And then there was the gap.

205
00:13:40,972 --> 00:13:45,513
After a series of helicopter escapes in the nineteen eighties and nineties,

206
00:13:45,563 --> 00:13:50,105
French prisons had been installing anti helicopter nets for years. At Reau,

207
00:13:50,155 --> 00:13:54,024
the main inner courtyard near the visiting area was not covered.

208
00:13:55,824 --> 00:13:57,700
The plan also needed a pilot.

209
00:13:57,750 --> 00:14:01,886
And investigators later believed this was not chosen at random.

210
00:14:03,686 --> 00:14:07,460
In April twenty nineteen, the pilot who had been taken hostage,

211
00:14:07,510 --> 00:14:11,466
his partner, and his stepdaughter were briefly taken into custody.

212
00:14:11,516 --> 00:14:16,565
Investigators had found that the stepdaughter had written letters to Faid in prison,

213
00:14:16,615 --> 00:14:19,175
between twenty fourteen and twenty sixteen.

214
00:14:20,975 --> 00:14:23,050
According to French press reports,

215
00:14:23,100 --> 00:14:28,113
investigators believed this connection may have helped the gang identify a pilot.

216
00:14:28,163 --> 00:14:30,801
But all three were released two days later.

217
00:14:30,851 --> 00:14:33,488
They were treated as victims, not suspects.

218
00:14:33,538 --> 00:14:36,301
The pilot had been taken hostage at gunpoint.

219
00:14:38,101 --> 00:14:40,605
One more detail made the plan possible.

220
00:14:40,655 --> 00:14:44,599
Faid's visiting room was close to the courtyard with no nets.

221
00:14:44,649 --> 00:14:46,564
When the helicopter came down,

222
00:14:46,614 --> 00:14:48,986
he was exactly where he needed to be.

223
00:14:51,236 --> 00:14:52,875
Chapter 7. The hunt.

224
00:14:59,225 --> 00:15:01,980
France reacted with shock, and with anger.

225
00:15:02,030 --> 00:15:06,054
Prison unions pointed straight at the courtyard with no nets.

226
00:15:07,854 --> 00:15:11,366
The police issued a wanted notice, with Faid's photo.

227
00:15:11,416 --> 00:15:16,272
Officers watched his family, his old neighborhoods, his known associates.

228
00:15:18,072 --> 00:15:21,394
On July twenty fourth, three weeks after the escape,

229
00:15:21,444 --> 00:15:25,739
gendarmes spotted him north of Paris. According to French accounts,

230
00:15:25,789 --> 00:15:31,251
Faid and his brother Rachid were seen near Sarcelles, apparently scouting businesses.

231
00:15:31,301 --> 00:15:33,521
They fled, and abandoned their car.

232
00:15:35,321 --> 00:15:38,945
Inside, investigators found what appeared to be explosives.

233
00:15:38,995 --> 00:15:42,058
Accounts differ on whether they were real or fake.

234
00:15:42,108 --> 00:15:45,421
They also found everyday supplies, and mosquito spray,

235
00:15:45,471 --> 00:15:48,784
suggesting the men had been hiding in the countryside.

236
00:15:50,584 --> 00:15:54,384
Then the trail went cold again. For weeks, there was nothing.

237
00:15:54,434 --> 00:15:58,044
The man who had escaped by helicopter had simply vanished.

238
00:16:01,494 --> 00:16:03,388
Chapter 8. The burqa.

239
00:16:09,738 --> 00:16:10,400
In the end,

240
00:16:10,450 --> 00:16:13,635
he had gone back to the place he knew best. Creil,

241
00:16:13,685 --> 00:16:15,382
the town where he was born.

242
00:16:17,182 --> 00:16:20,303
In the early hours of October third, twenty eighteen,

243
00:16:20,353 --> 00:16:24,191
at around four in the morning, police raided a building in Creil.

244
00:16:25,991 --> 00:16:29,894
Inside, they found Redoine Faid. He was wearing a burqa,

245
00:16:29,944 --> 00:16:34,271
a full face covering, to avoid being recognized in the street.

246
00:16:36,071 --> 00:16:39,585
He was arrested with his brother and two other men.

247
00:16:39,635 --> 00:16:43,567
Police seized weapons. After ninety four days on the run,

248
00:16:43,617 --> 00:16:47,061
the most wanted man in France was back in custody.

249
00:16:49,861 --> 00:16:53,749
This time, there would be no visiting room near an open courtyard.

250
00:16:53,799 --> 00:16:57,507
He was placed in strict isolation, under constant surveillance,

251
00:16:57,557 --> 00:16:59,894
and moved between high security prisons.

252
00:17:01,694 --> 00:17:05,452
In twenty twenty, he went on hunger strike to protest his conditions.

253
00:17:07,252 --> 00:17:11,075
The hunger strike began on February twentieth, twenty twenty,

254
00:17:11,125 --> 00:17:15,265
in protest at his conditions of isolation. For the prison service,

255
00:17:15,315 --> 00:17:19,456
a man who had escaped twice could not be treated like anyone else.

256
00:17:21,256 --> 00:17:22,095
That same year,

257
00:17:22,145 --> 00:17:26,129
at the appeal trial over the armored van robbery in northern France,

258
00:17:26,179 --> 00:17:29,866
he refused to leave his cell. The trial went ahead without him.

259
00:17:29,916 --> 00:17:33,128
The court increased his sentence to twenty eight years.

260
00:17:35,378 --> 00:17:37,678
Chapter 9. A French tradition.

261
00:17:44,028 --> 00:17:48,988
Redoine Faid was not the first prisoner in France to leave by helicopter.

262
00:17:49,038 --> 00:17:49,812
Far from it.

263
00:17:51,612 --> 00:17:53,590
In February nineteen eighty one,

264
00:17:53,640 --> 00:17:57,139
two prisoners escaped from Fleury Merogis by helicopter.

265
00:17:57,189 --> 00:18:00,561
It was one of the first escapes of its kind in France,

266
00:18:00,611 --> 00:18:02,399
and it would not be the last.

267
00:18:04,199 --> 00:18:07,215
In May nineteen eighty six, Michel Vaujour,

268
00:18:07,265 --> 00:18:09,568
a robber serving a long sentence,

269
00:18:09,618 --> 00:18:13,276
was lifted off the roof of La Sante prison in Paris.

270
00:18:13,326 --> 00:18:16,484
The helicopter was flown by his wife, Nadine,

271
00:18:16,534 --> 00:18:21,689
who had reportedly taken flying lessons. They landed on a football pitch,

272
00:18:21,739 --> 00:18:22,759
and drove away.

273
00:18:24,559 --> 00:18:26,714
In February nineteen ninety two,

274
00:18:26,764 --> 00:18:31,675
three prisoners escaped by helicopter from a prison in Lyon. Afterwards,

275
00:18:31,725 --> 00:18:36,774
steel cables were strung across the central yard, at five meter intervals.

276
00:18:38,574 --> 00:18:40,665
And then there was Pascal Payet.

277
00:18:40,715 --> 00:18:44,680
He escaped by helicopter from a prison near Aix en Provence,

278
00:18:44,730 --> 00:18:48,828
and later, again, from a prison in Grasse, in twenty oh seven.

279
00:18:50,628 --> 00:18:54,115
In twenty oh three, while he was himself on the run,

280
00:18:54,165 --> 00:19:01,054
Payet reportedly used a hijacked helicopter to free three other inmates from the same prison near Aix.

281
00:19:01,104 --> 00:19:02,347
In twenty oh seven,

282
00:19:02,397 --> 00:19:05,204
he was lifted out of the prison in Grasse.

283
00:19:05,254 --> 00:19:07,109
He was recaptured each time.

284
00:19:08,909 --> 00:19:09,824
By some counts,

285
00:19:09,874 --> 00:19:15,224
France has seen more helicopter escape attempts than any other country in the world.

286
00:19:15,274 --> 00:19:18,117
Each time, the nets got wider. And each time,

287
00:19:18,167 --> 00:19:19,853
someone looked for the gap.

288
00:19:23,303 --> 00:19:25,336
Chapter 10. The verdict.

289
00:19:31,686 --> 00:19:33,153
In twenty twenty three,

290
00:19:33,203 --> 00:19:36,847
the trial for the helicopter escape took place in Paris.

291
00:19:36,897 --> 00:19:41,200
Faid was tried alongside eleven other people, including relatives,

292
00:19:41,250 --> 00:19:42,717
accused of helping him.

293
00:19:44,517 --> 00:19:48,266
The prosecution went through the operation in detail.

294
00:19:48,316 --> 00:19:52,208
Seven minutes and thirty three seconds. No shots fired.

295
00:19:52,258 --> 00:19:54,143
A plan prepared for months.

296
00:19:55,943 --> 00:20:00,792
Prosecutors asked for twenty two years. In late October twenty twenty three,

297
00:20:00,842 --> 00:20:05,047
the court sentenced Redoine Faid to fourteen more years in prison.

298
00:20:06,847 --> 00:20:07,901
Under French law,

299
00:20:07,951 --> 00:20:12,446
escape sentences cannot be served at the same time as other sentences.

300
00:20:12,496 --> 00:20:15,888
They are added on top. According to French reporting,

301
00:20:15,938 --> 00:20:20,434
Faid cannot expect to be released before around twenty sixty. By then,

302
00:20:20,484 --> 00:20:22,706
he would be eighty eight years old.

303
00:20:24,506 --> 00:20:27,767
Faid's escape became the most famous of them all.

304
00:20:27,817 --> 00:20:31,009
Because it was so fast. Because nobody was hurt.

305
00:20:31,059 --> 00:20:33,982
And because he had told everyone, for years,

306
00:20:34,032 --> 00:20:36,616
that he had learned it from the movies.

307
00:20:38,416 --> 00:20:40,839
To some, he is a criminal mastermind.

308
00:20:40,889 --> 00:20:43,045
To the family of Aurelie Fouquet,

309
00:20:43,095 --> 00:20:47,322
he is simply the man responsible for the attack that killed her.

310
00:20:47,372 --> 00:20:49,528
And to the French prison service,

311
00:20:49,578 --> 00:20:53,271
he is a reminder that every wall, every net, every door,

312
00:20:53,321 --> 00:20:54,340
has a weak spot.

313
00:20:57,340 --> 00:21:00,406
So what do you think? Was this escape genius,

314
00:21:00,456 --> 00:21:03,106
or just a failure of the prison system?

315
00:21:03,156 --> 00:21:04,767
Tell us in the comments.

316
00:21:04,817 --> 00:21:08,713
And if you want to see the most secure prison in America,

317
00:21:08,763 --> 00:21:12,867
a place where an escape like this is meant to be impossible,

318
00:21:12,917 --> 00:21:15,290
watch our story about ADX Florence.
